{"test_id": "B04-001", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A power generation facility has the following systems: (1) SCADA system controlling turbine operations, (2) corporate email server, (3) historian database collecting sensor data from turbines, (4) HR payroll system, (5) distributed control system (DCS) managing plant operations. Classify each system as IT, OT, or hybrid based on CCoP 2.0 Section 10 definitions.", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "OT: SCADA, DCS; IT: email server, HR payroll; Hybrid: historian", "expected_response": "Based on the CCoP 2.0 §1.2.1 OT definition and §10.1.1 OT-CII scope: SCADA system and DCS are OT systems as they control physical processes and industrial operations. The corporate email server and HR payroll system are IT systems as they manage data and business operations. The historian database is a hybrid system - it collects OT sensor data but functions as an IT database system for data analysis and reporting. OT systems are characterized by direct interaction with physical processes, real-time requirements, and safety criticality. IT systems focus on data processing, business operations, and information management. Hybrid systems like historians bridge both domains by collecting OT data for IT-based analysis.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "OT systems control physical processes and industrial operations (SCADA, DCS)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "IT systems manage data and business operations (email, HR, payroll)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Hybrid systems like historians collect OT data for IT-based analysis", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Section 10 provides OT-specific security requirements recognizing different operational characteristics", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All systems in a power plant are OT because they support critical infrastructure", "Historian databases are pure IT systems with no OT considerations"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 defines specific percentage thresholds for hybrid classification", "Inventing a formal certification process for IT vs OT determination"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 10: Operational Technology Security", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1", "10.1.2"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "multi_system_classification", "related_sections": ["5.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["10.1", "10.1.1"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "PASS"}}
{"test_id": "B04-002", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A maritime port's vessel traffic management system combines radar sensors (OT), video analytics (IT), and automated collision detection algorithms to direct ship movements. Is this system classified as IT, OT, or hybrid under CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "maritime", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Hybrid IT/OT", "expected_response": "Under CCoP 2.0 §1.2.1 and §10.2.1, this is a mixed IT/OT environment whose OT components must be distinguished from connected IT components. The radar sensors and their direct connection to vessel movement controls constitute OT components. The video analytics processing and collision detection algorithms running on standard computing platforms constitute IT components. The system integrates physical process monitoring (OT) with data analytics and decision support (IT). CCoP 2.0 §10 recognizes that modern critical infrastructure systems increasingly blend IT and OT capabilities. Hybrid systems require consideration of both Section 5 controls for the IT-side components and §10 (OT security) requirements, with particular attention to the IT/OT boundary where data flows between domains.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Radar sensors connected to vessel movement controls are OT components", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Video analytics and collision detection algorithms on standard platforms are IT components", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Hybrid systems require both Section 5 and Section 10 security considerations", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The IT/OT boundary in hybrid systems requires special attention to data flows and access controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Maritime systems are always OT regardless of IT components", "Any system with sensors is automatically OT"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing a specific ratio threshold for hybrid classification", "Claiming maritime sector has unique IT/OT definitions not in CCoP"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 10: Operational Technology Security", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.2.1", "10.2.3"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "hybrid_system_identification", "related_sections": ["5.4.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-003", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Does a building management system (BMS) that controls HVAC, lighting, and fire safety systems count as IT or OT under CCoP 2.0? The BMS runs on standard servers but controls physical building equipment.", "scenario_sector": "government", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Hybrid IT/OT (OT-dominant)", "expected_response": "A building management system is classified as hybrid IT/OT with OT-dominant characteristics under CCoP 2.0. While the BMS software may run on standard IT servers (hardware), its function of controlling physical building equipment (HVAC actuators, lighting circuits, fire suppression systems) makes it an OT system from a security perspective. The key distinction is purpose: OT systems control physical processes, even when built on IT-like infrastructure. CCoP 2.0 §10.1 defines OT by function - monitoring and controlling physical devices and processes - not by hardware type. A BMS that directly commands actuators and safety systems falls under OT security requirements, with additional IT security considerations for the computing platform.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "OT systems are defined by their function of controlling physical processes, not hardware type", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "BMS controls physical equipment (HVAC, lighting, fire safety) making it OT by function", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Systems built on IT hardware but controlling physical processes are OT-dominant hybrids", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "OT security requirements apply based on system function regardless of underlying IT infrastructure", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Systems running on standard servers are always IT regardless of function", "BMS is pure IT because it uses standard operating systems"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing hardware-based classification criteria not in CCoP 2.0", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 exempts BMS from OT requirements based on server type"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 10: Operational Technology Security", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "hardware_platform_classification", "related_sections": ["5.3"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-004", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Our hospital uses IoT medical devices that connect to the hospital network for patient monitoring. These devices measure vital signs and transmit data to electronic health records (EHR) systems. Are these medical devices IT, OT, or hybrid under CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "healthcare", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Hybrid IT/OT", "expected_response": "Remove 'IT security (network protection, data encryption)' as a §5.4 reference. Anchor the IT/OT classification on §1.2.1 OT definition and §10.1.1 OT-CII scope. State that the IoT device's IT-side controls fall under Section 5 generally (Protection) without naming §5.4 specifically — or, if a specific clause is wanted, cite §5.1 (Access Control) or §5.13 (Cryptography) which actually cover those topics.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "IoT devices are classified as IT, OT, or hybrid based on their functions, not as a separate category", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Physical sensing functions represent OT characteristics", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Network connectivity and data transmission represent IT characteristics", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Hybrid IoT devices require both IT and OT security considerations", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All IoT devices are IT regardless of physical sensing function", "IoT is a separate category from IT/OT in CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing a dedicated IoT section in CCoP 2.0", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 has different rules for medical IoT vs other IoT"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "iot_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-005", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "An industrial manufacturing facility uses Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs) to control assembly line robots. The PLCs are programmed using engineering workstations on the corporate network, and operators monitor production via HMI interfaces. Which components are IT vs OT?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "PLCs/HMI: OT; Engineering workstations: IT (with OT access)", "expected_response": "Based on CCoP 2.0 Section 10: The PLCs are OT systems - they directly control physical industrial processes (assembly line robots). The HMI interfaces are OT systems - they provide direct human interaction with OT processes. The engineering workstations are IT systems by platform (standard computing), but they have special OT security considerations because they can program and modify PLC behavior. CCoP 2.CCoP 2.0 §10.2.1 restricts OT-CII to enterprise-network connections only as necessary for operation, with data flows one-way from OT-CII to enterprise. §10.2.3 additionally requires separate authentication mechanisms and account credentials for OT-CII vs enterprise users — preventing compromised credentials from being reused across boundaries. Engineering workstations therefore need credentials distinct from any enterprise-network credentials. §10.3.2 also requires the CIIO to verify integrity of programme codes in field controllers, which constrains how engineering workstations may modify PLC logic. Engineering workstations that can modify OT logic require particular attention - compromise of these IT systems could directly affect physical process safety. The classification affects security requirements: PLCs and HMIs follow OT-specific requirements, while engineering workstations follow IT requirements with additional controls for OT access.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "PLCs directly control industrial processes making them OT systems", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "HMI interfaces providing direct OT process interaction are OT systems", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Engineering workstations are IT systems with special OT access considerations", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "IT systems that can program OT devices require additional security controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.3.2", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Engineering workstations are OT because they connect to PLCs", "All systems on the factory floor are OT regardless of function"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific firewall rules not stated in CCoP 2.0", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 requires separate physical networks for programming"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 10: Operational Technology Security", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.2.1", "10.2.3", "10.3.2"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "industrial_control_classification", "related_sections": ["5.4.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["10.1", "10.2.3", "10.3.2"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "PARTIAL_ACCEPT"}}
{"test_id": "B04-006", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A smart grid system includes: (1) power distribution SCADA, (2) smart meters at customer premises, (3) meter data management system (MDMS) in the cloud, (4) customer web portal for viewing usage. Classify each component as IT, OT, or hybrid.", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "SCADA: OT; Smart meters: Hybrid; MDMS: IT; Web portal: IT", "expected_response": "Classification under CCoP 2.0: (1) Power distribution SCADA is OT - it directly controls physical electrical grid switching and equipment. (2) Smart meters are hybrid IT/OT - they have physical sensor functions (measuring power flow) and IT communication functions (transmitting data). (3) MDMS is IT - it manages meter data as information in cloud databases without controlling physical grid operations. (4) Customer web portal is IT - it provides information access and user interface without physical process control. CCoP 2.0 §10 applies to the SCADA system and has implications for smart meters (OT aspects). Section 5 controls apply to MDMS, web portal, and smart meter communication (IT aspects). The smart grid exemplifies how modern infrastructure has interconnected IT and OT components - each classified by function but requiring coordinated security across the IT/OT boundary.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "SCADA controlling physical grid switching is OT", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Smart meters combine physical sensing (OT) with data communication (IT)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "MDMS and web portals are IT systems managing data without physical control", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Smart grids require coordinated security across IT and OT components", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All smart grid components are OT because they support power infrastructure", "Smart meters are pure IT because they transmit digital data"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing smart grid-specific classification rules not in CCoP 2.0", "Claiming customer portals are OT because they display CII-related data"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "smart_grid_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-007", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Our water utility has a laboratory information management system (LIMS) that manages water quality testing data. Water samples are tested manually by technicians, who enter results into LIMS. The LIMS generates reports but does not control any treatment plant equipment. Is this IT or OT?", "scenario_sector": "water", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "IT", "expected_response": "The LIMS is an IT system under CCoP 2.0, not OT. While it supports water utility operations (a CII sector), the LIMS itself performs data management functions: storing test results, generating reports, and managing information workflows. It does not directly control any physical processes or equipment. The fact that water samples are tested manually and results are entered by technicians confirms there is no direct OT connection. Under CCoP 2.0 §1.2.1 and §10.1.1, systems that monitor and/or control physical processes are treated as OT. A LIMS that manages information about testing, without connection to automated treatment controls, falls under Section 5 controls for the IT-side components rather than §10 (OT security). If the LIMS were integrated with treatment plant controls to automatically adjust chemical dosing based on test results, it would become a hybrid or OT system.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "LIMS performs data management without controlling physical processes", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Manual sample testing and data entry confirms no direct OT connection", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Supporting CII operations does not make a system OT - function determines classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Integration with automated controls would change classification to hybrid/OT", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All water utility systems are OT because water is CII", "Any system supporting CII operations automatically falls under Section 10"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing CII sector-based automatic OT classification", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 treats all lab systems as OT"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "5.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "support_system_classification", "related_sections": ["10.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-008", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Does CCoP 2.0 Section 10's OT security requirements apply to a dam's floodgate control system that uses manual hydraulic controls with no digital or electronic components?", "scenario_sector": "water", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Not applicable - non-digital system", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 applies to computer systems and digital security. A purely mechanical floodgate control system with no digital or electronic components falls outside CCoP 2.0 scope entirely. CCoP 2.0 §10 addresses OT security for computer-based systems that monitor and control physical processes - SCADA, PLCs, digital controllers, industrial networks. A manual hydraulic system that operates without computers, software, or network connectivity is not subject to CCoP 2.0 cybersecurity requirements. However, if the dam has any digital systems (monitoring sensors, control room computers, communication systems) that support floodgate operations, those digital systems would fall under CCoP 2.0 scope. The question asks specifically about the floodgate control mechanism itself - if purely mechanical, it is not a CCoP 2.0 concern.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 applies to computer systems and digital security, not purely mechanical systems", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope, Section 1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Section 10 OT security covers computer-based industrial control systems", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Purely mechanical controls without digital components are outside CCoP 2.0 scope", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope definition", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Digital systems supporting physical operations would still be in scope", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Scope definition", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All dam infrastructure is subject to CCoP 2.0 regardless of technology", "Manual systems must comply with OT security requirements"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing mechanical system requirements in CCoP 2.0", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 has physical security mandates for manual equipment"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Scope", "clause_reference": ["1.3.1", "1.4.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "scope_boundary", "related_sections": ["10.1"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-009", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Our aviation navigation systems include ground-based radars (OT), radar data processing servers (IT), and air traffic control workstations (hybrid - operator interface to OT systems). Is this classification correct per CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "aviation", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Yes - Radars: OT; Processing servers: IT; ATC workstations: Hybrid", "expected_response": "The classification is correct under CCoP 2.0. Ground-based radars are OT systems - they directly sense and interact with the physical environment (aircraft position detection using radio waves). Radar data processing servers are IT systems - they perform data computation and information processing without directly controlling physical equipment. Air traffic control workstations are hybrid IT/OT - they are IT platforms (computers, displays, input devices) that provide human operator interface to OT systems (air traffic control commands that affect aircraft movements). The hybrid classification for ATC workstations recognizes that while the hardware is IT, the function is OT operational control. CCoP 2.0 §10 would apply to radars and have implications for ATC workstations. Section 5 would apply to radar processing servers. The IT/OT boundary between processing servers and radars, and between ATC workstations and radar systems, requires secure gateway implementation under §10.2.1 and, where relevant, §10.2.3.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Ground-based radars directly sensing physical environment are OT systems", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Data processing servers performing computation are IT systems", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "ATC workstations are hybrid - IT hardware providing OT operational control interface", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "IT/OT boundaries between components require secure gateways per Section 10.2.3", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Radar data processing servers are OT because they process radar data", "All aviation systems are OT because they affect flight safety"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing aviation-specific IT/OT rules not in CCoP 2.0", "Claiming safety-critical automatically means OT classification"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1", "10.2.1", "10.2.3"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "aviation_systems_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-010", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A telecom operator's 5G network includes: (1) cell tower radios and antennas, (2) RAN (Radio Access Network) controllers, (3) core network switches, (4) network management system. Classify each as IT or OT.", "scenario_sector": "telecoms", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Tower radios/antennas: OT; RAN controllers: Hybrid; Core switches: IT; NMS: IT", "expected_response": "Classification under CCoP 2.0: (1) Cell tower radios and antennas are OT systems - they directly transmit and receive radio frequency signals as part of physical network infrastructure. While not traditional 'industrial' OT, they are physical network equipment with direct environmental interaction. (2) RAN controllers are hybrid IT/OT - they bridge physical radio equipment (OT) with packet data processing (IT). (3) Core network switches are IT systems - they perform data routing and switching as network infrastructure. (4) Network management system is IT - it provides monitoring and configuration management without directly controlling physical RF transmission. CCoP 2.0 §10 primarily targets traditional industrial OT (SCADA, PLCs), but telecom physical infrastructure has OT characteristics. Core network and management are clearly IT. RAN controllers and cell towers represent the IT/OT boundary in telecom infrastructure.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Cell tower radios and antennas directly interface with physical RF spectrum - OT characteristics", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "RAN controllers bridge physical radio (OT) and data processing (IT)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Core network switches and NMS are IT systems", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Telecom physical infrastructure has OT characteristics even if not traditional industrial control", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All telecom equipment is IT because it handles digital data", "Only SCADA/PLC systems count as OT under CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing telecom-specific IT/OT definitions", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 Section 10 does not apply to telecom physical infrastructure"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "telecom_infrastructure_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-011", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A railway signaling system uses track circuits to detect train presence and switches to control train routing. The system includes wayside signal equipment (OT), traffic management center servers (IT), and centralized traffic control workstations. What is the correct classification?", "scenario_sector": "transport", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Wayside signals: OT; TMC servers: IT; CTC workstations: Hybrid", "expected_response": "Classification under CCoP 2.0: Wayside signal equipment (track circuits, switches, signals) are OT systems - they directly sense and control physical train movements and routing. Traffic management center servers are IT systems - they process scheduling data, manage information, and support decision-making without directly controlling physical equipment. Centralized traffic control workstations are hybrid IT/OT - they are IT computing platforms that provide human operator interface to OT signaling systems. This follows the same pattern as air traffic control: the computers are IT, the function is operational control. CCoP 2.0 Section 10 applies to wayside signaling equipment. Section 5 applies to TMC servers. CTC workstations require both IT security (as computers) and OT security considerations (as interfaces to safety-critical control systems). Per §10.2.1, OT-CII (wayside signalling) connections to any enterprise network must be limited to operational necessity with one-way data flow OT→enterprise. §10.2.3 additionally requires separate authentication mechanisms and credentials between the two networks, preventing reuse of compromised credentials..", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Wayside signaling equipment directly controls train movements - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Traffic management servers perform data processing - IT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CTC workstations are hybrid - IT hardware with OT control interface function", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "IT/OT boundary segmentation required per Section 10.2.3", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All railway systems are OT because they control trains", "TMC servers are OT because they support train operations"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing railway-specific classification exceptions", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 has different rules for signaling vs other OT"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1", "10.2.1", "10.2.3"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "railway_signaling_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-012", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Our CII organization uses Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) software to manage spare parts inventory for OT equipment maintenance. The ERP does not control any OT equipment directly, but OT technicians use it to order parts. Is this IT or OT?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "IT", "expected_response": "When the Expected Response says 'falls under Section 5 (IT security)', remove implicit attribution to §5.4. Explicitly anchor on §10.1.1 (Section 10 limited to OT-CII) and §1.2.1 (OT-by-function definition), then cite §5.1 (Access Control) rather than §5.4 for the ERP's IT-security applicability.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "ERP performs business functions (inventory, procurement) without direct physical process control", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Supporting OT operations does not make a system OT - function determines classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "User base (OT technicians) does not change system classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "IT/OT integration creates boundary considerations but doesn't change core classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Any system used by OT staff is OT", "Systems supporting OT maintenance fall under Section 10"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing 'OT support system' classification category", "Claiming ERP systems need OT-specific security when used by OT"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "5.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "business_support_classification", "related_sections": ["10.2.3"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-013", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Does CCoP 2.0 classify an industrial firewall as IT or OT equipment? The firewall sits between the corporate IT network and the production OT network, filtering traffic between zones.", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Hybrid IT/OT (boundary device)", "expected_response": "An industrial firewall is classified as hybrid IT/OT under CCoP 2.0 - it is a boundary device that operates at the IT/OT interface. While the firewall hardware and software are IT in nature (standard computing platform, firewall software), its function is specifically OT security: enforcing the segmentation boundary required by Section 10.2.3. Industrial firewalls often have OT-specific features: understanding industrial protocols (Modbus, DNP3), deeper packet inspection for ICS traffic, and safety availability requirements. CCoP 2.0 §10.2.1 requires that OT-CII not be connected to any enterprise network except where necessary for operating the CII, and that data flows from OT-CII to enterprise networks be restricted to one-way. §10.2.2 requires monitoring those data flows for anomalies. An industrial firewall is the typical implementation choice for satisfying these requirements; the Code itself does not mandate any specific technology or use the term \"secure gateway\".. The classification affects how the device is secured: it needs IT security (firehardening, updates) as an IT device, plus OT-specific considerations (availability, protocol awareness, change control for safety impacts). Compromise of an IT/OT boundary firewall could provide access to OT systems, making it a critical security control.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Industrial firewalls are boundary devices operating at the IT/OT interface", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Firewall hardware/software is IT in nature but function is OT security", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Boundary firewalls implement Section 10.2.3 IT/OT segmentation requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Compromise of boundary firewalls could provide OT system access", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Firewalls are always IT regardless of deployment location", "Industrial firewalls are OT because they protect OT systems"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing separate firewall certification requirements for IT vs OT", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 mandates specific firewall brands for IT/OT boundaries"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 10: Operational Technology Security", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.2.1", "10.2.2"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "boundary_device_classification", "related_sections": ["5.4.1"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-014", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A bank's data center uses uninterruptible power supplies (UPS) and automated transfer switches for electrical redundancy. The power management system can automatically switch between utility power and generators. Is this power management system IT or OT?", "scenario_sector": "banking", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "OT (facility infrastructure)", "expected_response": "The power management system (UPS, automated transfer switches) is classified as OT under CCoP 2.0. While located in a banking data center (an IT environment), these systems directly control physical electrical infrastructure - a critical physical process. The UPS systems manage battery power conversion, and transfer switches physically route electrical power between sources. This is physical process control, even if the supported load is IT equipment. CCoP 2.0 Section 10 applies to OT systems including facility infrastructure that uses industrial control methods. Automated power switching is an OT function: it controls physical equipment and has safety implications (electrical fire hazards, equipment damage from improper switching). While a banking data center's core business systems are IT, the facility power infrastructure that keeps those systems running is OT. This classification means power management systems follow Section 10 requirements — notably §10.2.4, which requires that the OT-CII has fail-safes to ensure the safety and reliability of operations in the event of a cybersecurity incident. Power management.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Power management systems control physical electrical infrastructure - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "UPS and transfer switches perform physical process control (power routing)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Facility infrastructure using industrial controls is OT regardless of sector", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "OT classification applies even when the supported load is IT equipment", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All banking systems are IT regardless of function", "Power infrastructure is IT because it serves IT equipment"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing banking sector exemption from OT requirements", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 treats data centers differently than other facilities"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 10: Operational Technology Security", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1", "10.2.4"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "facility_infrastructure_classification", "related_sections": ["5.6"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-015", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Our manufacturing execution system (MES) tracks production orders, manages work-in-progress inventory, and provides production dashboards. It does not directly control machines - operators read dashboards and manually adjust equipment. Is MES IT or OT?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "IT (production support system)", "expected_response": "When asserting 'falls under Section 5 (IT security)', remove implicit reliance on §5.4 — keep §5.1 (Access Control) as the specific anchor and Section 5 in general for the broader IT control set. Anchor OT definition on §1.2.1.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "MES performs information management without direct physical process control", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Human operators interpreting dashboards is not direct OT monitoring/control", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Manufacturing support systems are IT when they manage data, not physical processes", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Automated integration with production equipment would create hybrid classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All manufacturing systems are OT", "MES is OT because it's used on the factory floor"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing 'production IT' category separate from IT/OT", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 treats MES differently than other data management systems"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "5.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "manufacturing_support_classification", "related_sections": ["10.2.3"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-016", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A port's crane operations use remote-controlled gantry cranes. Operators in a control room use joysticks and screens to move cranes. The crane control system includes: (1) crane-mounted PLCs, (2) radio link for control signals, (3) operator control stations, (4) scheduling software for container assignments. Classify each component.", "scenario_sector": "maritime", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Crane PLCs: OT; Radio link: OT; Control stations: Hybrid; Scheduling software: IT", "expected_response": "Classification under CCoP 2.0: (1) Crane-mounted PLCs are OT - they directly control crane motors and physical cargo movement. (2) Radio link for control signals is OT - it's the physical communications medium for OT control commands (part of OT network infrastructure). (3) Operator control stations are hybrid - IT hardware (computers, displays, joysticks) providing direct OT control interface. (4) Scheduling software is IT - it manages information about container assignments without directly controlling crane movements. This illustrates how OT systems have multiple layers: direct physical control (PLCs), communications (radio link), human interface (control stations), and planning/coordination (scheduling software). CCoP 2.0 §10 applies to PLCs, radio link, and has implications for control stations. Section 5 controls apply to scheduling software. IT/OT boundary considerations apply to data flows between scheduling software (IT) and crane control systems (OT).", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Crane-mounted PLCs directly control physical cargo movement - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "OT communication links (radio for control signals) are part of OT infrastructure", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Operator control stations are hybrid - IT hardware with direct OT control interface", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Scheduling and planning software is IT - manages information without physical control", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Scheduling software is OT because it affects crane operations", "Radio links are IT because they transmit digital data"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing port-specific IT/OT classification rules", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 exempts radio links from OT requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1", "10.2.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "port_crane_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-017", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Our oil refinery uses process control computers (PCCs) that run control software on Windows-based industrial PCs. These PCCs directly connect to refinery equipment via I/O cards. They are not standard office PCs - they are ruggedized with special I/O. Are they IT or OT?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "OT (Windows-based OT controller)", "expected_response": "Process control computers are OT systems under CCoP 2.0, despite running Windows. The key classification factor is function, not operating system. PCCs directly control refinery equipment through I/O cards - they execute control logic, read sensors, and command actuators. This is physical process control, which defines OT per CCoP 2.0 §10.1. While Windows is typically an IT operating system, CCoP 2.0 does not classify by OS type. OT systems increasingly use general-purpose computing platforms (Windows, Linux) rather than proprietary OT OS. What matters is the function: direct interaction with physical processes. The PCCs require OT security considerations: availability for safe operations, strict change control (updates could affect process safety), and protection from dangerous states. The Windows OS layer also needs IT security (patching, hardening), but these must be applied with OT-specific caution for safety and availability.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "OT classification is based on function (physical process control), not operating system", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "PCCs directly control refinery equipment through I/O cards - OT function", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "OT systems increasingly use general-purpose OS (Windows, Linux) rather than proprietary OT OS", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10, Industry context", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Windows-based OT controllers need both IT and OT security considerations", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All Windows-based systems are IT regardless of function", "OT systems must use proprietary or real-time operating systems"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing OS-based classification criteria in CCoP 2.0", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 prohibits Windows in OT environments"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 10: Operational Technology Security", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1", "10.2.4", "10.3.2"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "os_platform_classification", "related_sections": ["5.6"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-018", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A CII organization's security operations center (SOC) monitors both IT and OT systems. The SOC uses SIEM tools to collect logs from: (1) corporate servers (IT), (2) firewalls (IT), (3) SCADA systems (OT), (4) PLCs (OT). How is the SIEM classified?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "IT (monitoring system with OT data sources)", "expected_response": "The SIEM (Security Information and Event Management) system is classified as IT under CCoP 2.0. While it collects logs from both IT and OT sources, the SIEM itself performs data management and analysis functions - it is not directly controlling physical processes. CCoP 2.0 Section 10 applies to systems that monitor and control physical processes. The SIEM monitors security events (information about systems), not the physical processes themselves. This distinction is important: a SCADA system monitoring pipeline pressure is OT; a SIEM collecting logs from that SCADA system is IT. The SIEM's OT data sources create IT/OT boundary considerations: how logs are transmitted from OT to IT, what filtering is applied to protect OT from IT-side threats, and whether the SIEM could be used as a pivot to attack OT systems. But the SIEM itself remains an IT security monitoring tool per Section 6 (Detection), not an OT control system per Section 10.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "SIEM performs data management and analysis - IT classification regardless of data sources", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 6", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Monitoring security events (information about systems) vs monitoring physical processes", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 6, 10", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "OT data sources create IT/OT boundary considerations but don't change SIEM classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "SIEM could be used as pivot to attack OT - boundary protection required", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["SIEM is OT because it collects OT logs", "Any system monitoring OT data is automatically OT"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing 'OT monitoring' category separate from IT/OT", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 requires separate SIEMs for IT and OT"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 6: Detection", "clause_reference": ["6.2.1", "6.1.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "monitoring_system_classification", "related_sections": ["10.2.3"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-019", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Our transport sector CII uses automatic ticket gates at train stations. The gates include: (1) physical barrier mechanisms, (2) card readers, (3) gate controllers, (4) central fare management server. Classify each component.", "scenario_sector": "transport", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Barrier mechanisms: OT (electromechanical); Card readers: Hybrid; Gate controllers: OT; Fare server: IT", "expected_response": "Classification under CCoP 2.0: (1) Physical barrier mechanisms are OT - they are electromechanical equipment directly controlling physical access. (2) Card readers are hybrid - they combine physical sensing (reading card data) with IT communication (transmitting to fare server). (3) Gate controllers are OT - they directly command barrier open/close operations. (4) Central fare management server is IT - it processes fare transactions, manages account data, and supports business operations without directly controlling physical gates. The automatic ticket gate system illustrates IT/OT integration: IT systems (fare server) make business logic decisions about fare payment, while OT systems (gate controllers, barriers) execute physical access control. Card readers sit at the IT/OT boundary, bridging physical card interaction with digital transaction processing. CCoP 2.0 §10 applies to gate controllers and barrier mechanisms. Section 5 controls apply to fare server. Card readers require both IT and OT considerations.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Physical barrier mechanisms are electromechanical OT equipment", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Gate controllers directly command physical barrier operations - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Card readers are hybrid - physical sensing (OT) with IT communication", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Fare management server is IT - processes transactions without physical control", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All ticketing systems are IT because they handle payments", "Gate controllers are IT because they process digital fare data"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing transport-specific IT/OT rules", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 treats payment processing as inherently OT"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "ticket_gate_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-020", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A media company's broadcasting infrastructure includes: (1) video cameras, (2) video switchers for live production, (3) broadcast transmitters, (4) media asset management system. Classify each as IT or OT.", "scenario_sector": "media", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Cameras: OT; Video switchers: OT; Transmitters: OT; MAM: IT", "expected_response": "Classification under CCoP 2.0: (1) Video cameras are OT - they directly sense and capture physical visual signals (light from scenes). (2) Video switchers for live production are OT - they directly control the routing of video signals, affecting real-time broadcast output. (3) Broadcast transmitters are OT - they directly generate and transmit RF signals, a physical process. (4) Media asset management system is IT - it manages metadata, archives, and retrieval of media files as information without directly controlling transmission equipment. While media may not be traditional 'industrial' OT, broadcasting infrastructure involves physical signal generation and transmission (RF waves), which is physical process control. Video switchers directly affect what gets transmitted - they have real-time physical output implications. CCoP 2.0 §10 applies to cameras, switchers, and transmitters. Section 5 controls apply to the MAM system. If any of these systems are designated as CII, the respective security requirements apply.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Video cameras directly sense physical visual signals - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Video switchers and transmitters directly control physical signal routing/transmission", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Media asset management performs information management without physical control - IT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Broadcasting infrastructure involves physical process control (RF signal generation)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Media systems are always IT because they handle content", "Broadcasting is not 'real' OT like manufacturing or energy"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing media sector exemption from OT classification", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 defines OT differently for broadcasting"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "broadcasting_infrastructure_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-021", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Does CCoP 2.0 classify a drone used for industrial inspection as IT, OT, or hybrid? The drone has: onboard flight controller (OT), camera sensor (OT), wireless link to ground station, and ground control software running on a laptop.", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Flight controller: OT; Camera: OT; Wireless link: OT; Ground software: Hybrid", "expected_response": "Classification under CCoP 2.0: (1) Onboard flight controller is OT - it directly controls physical drone motors and flight surfaces. (2) Camera sensor is OT - it directly captures physical visual data. (3) Wireless link is OT - it's the physical communication medium for OT control and sensor data. (4) Ground control software is hybrid - IT platform (laptop) providing direct OT control interface (flight commands). Industrial drones are OT systems from a CCoP 2.0 perspective because they directly control physical equipment (motors, gimbals) and interact with the physical environment (sensing). The laptop running ground software is IT hardware, but its function is OT operational control - making it hybrid like other operator interface stations. CCoP 2.0 §10 applies to the flight controller, camera, wireless link, and has implications for ground software. Section 5 controls apply to the laptop as IT equipment. If the drone is used for CII inspection tasks, the OT components likely fall within CII scope for cybersecurity compliance.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Flight controller directly controls drone motors - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Camera sensor directly captures physical data - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Ground control software on laptop is hybrid - IT hardware with OT control interface", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Industrial drones used for CII inspection are OT systems for CCoP 2.0 compliance", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Drones are IT because they're 'consumer electronics'", "Ground control software is pure IT because it runs on a laptop"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing drone-specific classification rules", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 treats drones differently than other OT equipment"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "drone_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-022", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A healthcare provider has a patient monitoring system that: (1) collects vital signs from bedside sensors, (2) displays patient data at nurse stations, (3) generates alerts for critical values, (4) stores historical data in electronic health records. Classify each component.", "scenario_sector": "healthcare", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Bedside sensors: OT; Display system: Hybrid; Alerting system: Hybrid; EHR storage: IT", "expected_response": "Classification under CCoP 2.0: (1) Bedside sensors are OT - they directly sense physical patient physiology (heart rate, blood pressure, oxygen saturation). (2) Display system at nurse stations is hybrid - IT platform (computers, screens) displaying OT-derived patient data. (3) Alerting system is hybrid - it combines OT sensor input with IT notification (pager, smartphone alerts). (4) EHR historical data storage is IT - it manages information records without real-time patient monitoring connection. Patient monitoring systems illustrate IT/OT convergence in healthcare. The physical sensing layer (sensors connected to patients) is OT - direct interaction with physical processes. The data presentation and storage layers are IT - information management and display. The alerting system bridges both worlds: OT triggers (sensor readings exceeding thresholds) generating IT notifications (pager messages). Under CCoP 2.0 §1.2.1, the bedside sensors are the clearest OT component; connected display, alerting and record-storage systems remain IT or hybrid components that must be secured consistently with the OT boundary and the applicable Section 5 controls.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Bedside sensors directly measure patient physiology - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Nurse station displays are hybrid - IT hardware showing OT-derived data", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Alerting systems bridge OT triggers with IT notifications", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "EHR data storage is IT - manages information without direct physical sensing", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All healthcare systems are IT because they handle patient data", "Patient monitoring is not 'real' OT like industrial control"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing healthcare-specific IT/OT definitions", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 exempts medical devices from OT requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "patient_monitoring_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-023", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "Our government agency uses physical access control systems: (1) door readers (card/biometric), (2) door controllers that lock/unlock doors, (3) access management server that stores permissions, (4) monitoring dashboard showing door events. Classify each component.", "scenario_sector": "government", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Door readers: Hybrid; Door controllers: OT; Access server: IT; Monitoring dashboard: IT", "expected_response": "Classification under CCoP 2.0: (1) Door readers are hybrid - they combine physical sensing (reading cards/biometrics) with IT communication (transmitting to access server). (2) Door controllers are OT - they directly command physical door locking mechanisms. (3) Access management server is IT - it stores permission data, processes access requests, and manages information without directly controlling physical doors. (4) Monitoring dashboard is IT - it displays historical access event information without real-time door control. Physical access control systems have clear IT/OT separation: the decision layer (access server) is IT - processing business logic about permissions. The execution layer (door controllers) is OT - directly controlling physical door hardware. Door readers sit at the boundary, bridging physical interaction (card/biometric presentation) with digital authentication. CCoP 2.0 §10 applies to door controllers. Section 5 controls apply to access server and monitoring dashboard. Door readers require both IT and OT security considerations.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Door controllers directly command physical door locks - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Access management server processes permissions - IT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Door readers are hybrid - physical sensing with IT communication", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Physical access control has IT decision layer and OT execution layer", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["All access control systems are IT because they manage digital permissions", "Door controllers are IT because they receive digital commands"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing physical security specific IT/OT rules", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 treats door controllers as IT because they're 'security' equipment"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "access_control_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-024", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "A data center uses environmental control systems: (1) temperature sensors in server racks, (2) HVAC controllers that adjust cooling, (3) building automation system (BAS) server, (4) leak detection sensors under raised floors. Classify each as IT or OT.", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "Temp sensors: OT; HVAC controllers: OT; BAS server: Hybrid; Leak sensors: OT", "expected_response": "Classification under CCoP 2.0: (1) Temperature sensors are OT - they directly sense physical environmental conditions (heat in server racks). (2) HVAC controllers are OT - they directly control physical cooling equipment (fans, compressors, chillers). (3) BAS server is hybrid - IT platform (standard server) providing OT monitoring and control interface. (4) Leak detection sensors are OT - they directly sense physical water presence. Data center environmental controls are OT systems despite being in an IT facility. They monitor and control physical processes: temperature regulation, humidity control, water detection. The BAS server is IT hardware running OT management software - similar to SCADA servers in industrial plants. CCoP 2.0 §10 applies to sensors, HVAC controllers, and has implications for the BAS server. Section 5 controls apply to the BAS server as IT equipment. This classification affects how environmental controls are secured: availability is critical (IT equipment overheats without cooling), and unsafe states must be prevented (excessive cooling can cause equipment damage from condensation).", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Temperature and leak sensors directly sense physical conditions - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "HVAC controllers directly control physical cooling equipment - OT classification", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "BAS server is hybrid - IT hardware with OT monitoring/control function", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Environmental controls are OT regardless of being in an IT facility", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Data center environmental systems are IT because they serve IT equipment", "Sensors are IT because they transmit digital data"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing data center exemption from OT classification", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 treats facility systems differently based on served equipment"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "10.1.1", "10.2.4"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "data_center_environmental_classification", "related_sections": ["6.2.1"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B04-025", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B04", "input": {"question": "For IT/OT classification purposes under CCoP 2.0, what is the primary determining factor: (a) the operating system used, (b) the physical location of the equipment, (c) the function the system performs, or (d) the user base who operates it?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "(c) The function the system performs", "expected_response": "The primary determining factor for IT/OT classification under CCoP 2.0 is the function the system performs. CCoP 2.0 §1.2.1 (Glossary) defines OT as an arrangement of interconnected computers used in the monitoring and/or control of physical processes, including SCADA, DCS, and PLCs, and industrial control components achieving an industrial objective. CCoP 2.0 does not provide a separate explicit \"IT\" definition; Section 5 (Protection) sets out IT-security requirements that apply to CII generally, with Section 10 narrowing/adding requirements specifically for OT-CII per §10.1.1. The function-based test follows from the §1.2.1 OT definition: if a system monitors/controls physical processes, it is OT; otherwise it falls under Section 5 IT-protection requirements (with hybrid systems satisfying both where each function applies). The classification is not determined by operating system (Windows vs real-time OS), physical location (data center vs factory floor), or user base (IT staff vs OT technicians). While these factors may correlate with IT or OT systems, they are not the defining characteristic. A Windows-based system in a data center can be OT if it directly controls physical processes. A Linux system on a factory floor can be IT if it performs data analysis. The key question is: Does this system directly monitor or control physical processes (OT) or does it manage information and data (IT)?", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 classifies by function, not OS, location, or users", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "OT systems directly monitor/control physical processes per Section 10.1", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "IT systems manage data and information per Section 5", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Hybrid systems perform both IT and OT functions", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5, 10", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["OS type determines IT vs OT classification", "Physical location (factory floor vs office) is the deciding factor"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing classification criteria not based on function", "Claiming CCoP 2.0 uses OS, location, or user base for classification"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Sections 5 and 10", "clause_reference": ["1.2.1", "5.1", "10.1.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "classification_principle", "related_sections": ["10.1.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
